const lifecycle=['Project intelligence','PE intelligence','Gap & conflict review','Workload model','Performance Contract','Strategy & test plan','Canonical test definition','k6 execution','Results','Acceptance','Findings','Evidence package','Publishing / export'];
---
<BaseLayout title="Performance Engineering Control Plane | Software Product Case Study | Wear Valley Digital" description="PECP is an enterprise Performance Engineering platform being developed by Wear Valley Digital to transform incomplete project intelligence into governed workload models, executable tests and traceable engineering evidence." canonical={canonical} structuredData={structuredData}>
<article class="case-study pecp">
  <header class="case-hero" id="top"><div class="wrap"><nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/work/">Work</a> / PECP</nav><p class="case-kicker">Enterprise Performance Engineering software</p><h1>Performance Engineering starts with questions, not scripts.</h1><p class="lede">PECP converts messy project intelligence into governed, traceable Performance Engineering decisions—from business demand to engineering evidence.</p><p class="status-line"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Product under active development.</p></div></header>

  <section class="case-section"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>Generating load is not the hardest part</h2><p>Requirements, architecture, tickets, spreadsheets, telemetry and stakeholder conversations rarely arrive as one dependable performance model. Engineers still have to find the information, judge its provenance, expose gaps and conflicts, translate demand into workload, design a valid experiment and defend the final decision.</p><p>PECP is being built to govern that process. It sits above the Performance Engineering toolchain rather than replacing k6, JMeter, delivery systems, observability platforms or work-management products.</p></div><aside class="panel"><p class="quote">How do I turn incomplete business information into a defensible engineering decision?</p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="case-section dark"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">100,000 users</p><h2>That is not yet a performance requirement.</h2><p>Are they registered, daily active or concurrent users? Which journeys do they perform, how often, and during what peak? What throughput matters? Which figures are approved, and which are still assumptions?</p></div><aside><p class="metric">100,000</p><p class="lede">sounds precise, but an execution engine still does not know what workload to generate.</p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="case-section alt"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">One governed lifecycle</p><h2>From project intelligence to published evidence</h2><ol class="flow" aria-label="PECP lifecycle">{lifecycle.map((item,index)=><li><span class="label">{String(index+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><br>{item}</li>)}</ol><p class="section-intro">PECP treats these stages as one governed engineering lifecycle rather than a collection of disconnected documents and tools.</p></div></section>

  <section class="case-section"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Canonical model</p><h2>Missing information remains missing</h2><p>Documents, portal views, tests, reports and integrations connect to a governed Performance Engineering model. Source, state, history and calculation lineage answer a vital question: <em>where did this come from?</em></p><p>PECP does not invent a convenient default to make a workflow look complete. Workload calculations, acceptance, findings and evidence generation are deterministic.</p></div><aside class="panel"><p class="quote">Gather intelligence. Model reality. Design the experiment. Verify the evidence. Make the decision.</p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="case-section dark"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">RetailCo reference lab</p><h2>Translating business demand into workload</h2><p class="section-intro">A fictional Black Friday 2026 scenario verifies the lifecycle end to end.</p><div class="grid"><div><p class="metric">31,500</p><p>orders per hour</p></div><div><p class="metric">8.75</p><p>orders per second</p></div><div><p class="metric">8%</p><p>checkout share of journeys</p></div><div><p class="metric">109.375</p><p>mixed iterations per second required</p></div></div><div class="journey-mix" aria-label="Journey distribution: Browse 55%, Search 20%, Basket 15%, Checkout 8%, Account 2%"><span style="flex:55">Browse 55%</span><span style="flex:20">Search 20%</span><span style="flex:15">Basket 15%</span><span style="flex:8">Checkout 8%</span><span style="flex:2">Account 2%</span></div><p>The business rate cannot be sent directly to a mixed-journey scheduler. PECP applies the journey distribution so the desired checkout throughput is represented by the whole workload.</p></div></section>

  <section class="case-section alt"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Acceptance that asks the harder question</p><h2>Thresholds passed. Workload proof did not.</h2><p>In the reference execution, individual criteria such as checkout p95 and HTTP failure rate passed. But the available telemetry could not conclusively prove that the required steady-state workload was achieved.</p><p>PECP therefore did not turn green thresholds into an unjustified success.</p></div><aside class="decision"><p class="label">Deterministic decision</p><p class="metric">INCONCLUSIVE</p><p><strong>Passing thresholds does not prove a valid performance test if the required workload was not proven.</strong></p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="case-section"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Capabilities established</p><h2>A platform, not a dashboard</h2><ul class="feature-list"><li><strong>Intelligence</strong><br>Canonical states, conflicts, provenance, intents and workload modelling.</li><li><strong>Contracts and artefacts</strong><br>Performance Contract, strategy, test plan, fingerprint binding and drift detection.</li><li><strong>Execution preparation</strong><br>Engine-neutral test definitions, k6 bundles, preconditions and workload schedules.</li><li><strong>Evidence governance</strong><br>Results, acceptance, findings, defect candidates, evidence and publication bundles.</li><li><strong>Portal</strong><br>Results, findings, executions, evidence and workload visualisation.</li><li><strong>Persistent platform</strong><br>Organisations, projects, migrations, tenancy boundaries, transactions and isolated requests.</li></ul></div></section>

  <section class="case-section dark"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Platform architecture</p><h2>Determinism through every layer</h2><div class="architecture"><article><p class="label">Portal</p><h3>React + TypeScript</h3><p>Vite, Tailwind and Recharts support structured review and workload visualisation.</p></article><article><p class="label">API and domain</p><h3>Node.js + Fastify</h3><p>Versioned services sit over deterministic domain packages and canonical engines.</p></article><article><p class="label">Persistence and execution</p><h3>SQLite + k6</h3><p>Transactional embedded persistence and an engine-neutral boundary with k6 first.</p></article></div><p>Repository and application-service boundaries, providers, a Unit of Work, concurrency isolation, npm workspaces and automated CI support reproducible behaviour.</p></div></section>

  <section class="case-section alt"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">AI-assisted engineering</p><h2>Acceleration with explicit authority boundaries</h2><p>PECP demonstrates an AI-assisted workflow in which AI accelerates implementation while architecture, domain constraints, tests and deterministic governance control the result. The same principle shapes the product: AI may assist extraction, classification, explanation and drafting, but it does not own calculation, approval or evidence.</p></div><aside class="panel"><p class="quote">AI proposes.<br>Systems verify.<br>Humans decide.</p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="case-section"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Current product status</p><h2>Built incrementally through governed milestones</h2><p>The deterministic Performance Engineering engine, evidence lifecycle, portal and persistent platform foundation are established. Current productisation work is adding identity, permissions, audit authority and the remaining customer-facing integration layers required for the sellable MVP.</p><p>The intended production model is customer-controlled deployment, not a conventional public multi-tenant SaaS. Existing enterprise customers and production readiness are not claimed.</p></div><aside class="panel"><p class="label">Wear Valley Digital capability</p><p>PECP shows how specialist practitioner knowledge can become domain models, deterministic business logic, APIs, persistent software and a credible path towards a commercial product.</p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="case-section dark"><div class="wrap cta-panel"><p class="eyebrow">From specialist process to software</p><h2>Does a complex process still live in spreadsheets, documents and manual decisions?</h2><p>PECP began with a real industry problem and a body of specialist Performance Engineering knowledge. Wear Valley Digital is turning that expertise into a governed software product.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href="/legal/contact/">Discuss a software product</a><a class="text-link" href="#top">Review the PECP approach</a></div></div></section>
</article>
</BaseLayout>
